#!/usr/bin/env bash
# claude-corpus — measure what Claude Code sessions actually did, from the transcripts.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the hooks in claude/ assert measured numbers in their header comments
# ("blocking waits cost 12.8h over 8 weeks", "the Grep matcher fired ZERO times in 8
# weeks", "1,009 full-suite calls"). Those numbers decided real config changes, but the
# measurement was done out of band and only the conclusions were committed. Nothing in
# the repo could recompute them, so the claims aged into folklore the moment the corpus
# rotated out from under them. This is the missing measuring stick.
#
# Three properties of that failure shape the design:
#
#   1. PREDICATES ARE NOT RE-IMPLEMENTED HERE. A hook's fire rate is measured by running
#      THAT HOOK against a payload synthesized from the transcript. A jq copy of
#      `segment_is_full_suite` would drift from the shell original on its first edit and
#      then report confident numbers about a predicate that no longer exists.
#   2. THE CORPUS EXPIRES. cleanupPeriodDays is unset, so Claude Code's 30-day default
#      retention applies: a window is unrecoverable before anyone thinks to re-read it
#      (a repeated count here dropped 47 -> 32 files within minutes). `--ledger` appends
#      one dated row per metric to a committed file so windows accumulate. The ledger is
#      the durable artifact; this report is only the view.
#   3. TOOL DURATION IS NOT RECORDED. No Bash tool_result carries a duration field
#      (only WebFetch/WebSearch do), so every time figure here is a TIMESTAMP DELTA —
#      see `--definitions`, which prints the exact rule for every metric. The
#      definitions are part of the output because they are the part that was lost.
set -uo pipefail

SELF=${0##*/}
SCRIPT_DIR=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd)
REPO_DIR=$(cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/.." >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd)

die() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$SELF" "$*" >&2; exit 1; }
need() { command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "missing dependency: $1"; }

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
claude-corpus — what installed config actually fires, and what it costs

  claude-corpus [CORPUS_ROOT] [options]

  CORPUS_ROOT            dir of session transcripts (default: ~/.claude/projects)

  --config FILE          settings.json holding the installed inventory
                         (default: <repo>/claude/settings.json, else ~/.claude/settings.json)
  --hooks-dir DIR        dir holding the hook scripts to replay
                         (default: <repo>/claude, else ~/.claude)
  --skills-root DIR      dir to scan for installed SKILL.md (repeatable)
                         (default: <repo>/claude/marketplaces AND ~/.claude/plugins)
                         The default covers the repo's own plugins and the installed
                         tree. Pass this ONLY to add a marketplace kept elsewhere —
                         passing it at all REPLACES the default, so passing just the
                         repo dir reports 13 skills and calls the other ~45 uninstalled.
  --no-replay            skip the hook replay (report only what the logs state)
  --ledger [FILE]        append one dated row per metric to the ledger
                         (default: <repo>/docs/claude-corpus-ledger.tsv)
  --definitions          print the metric definitions and exit
  --json                 emit the raw metrics object instead of the report
USAGE
}

definitions() {
  cat <<'DEFS'
claude-corpus metric definitions
================================

The methodology behind the numbers in claude/*.sh headers was not committed and the
session that produced it has aged out of retention. These definitions are re-derived
from those header claims; they are a reading of them, not a recovery of them. Where a
number here disagrees with a header, suspect the definition first.

WALL CLOCK (every duration in this report)
  No Bash tool_result records a duration. A call's cost is therefore the delta between
  the `assistant` record carrying the tool_use block and the `user` record carrying the
  matching tool_result (paired on tool_use_id). Consequences, all of them real:
    - it is ELAPSED time, and so includes hook execution and human approval latency;
    - a call with no matching result (interrupted, or last in the file) counts toward
      call totals and contributes zero time; it is reported separately;
    - timestamps are ISO-8601 with milliseconds, which jq's fromdateiso8601 rejects,
      so they are parsed by hand to millisecond precision.
  Where an assistant message carries SEVERAL tool_use blocks they share one start
  timestamp and ran concurrently, so summing them double-counts. This corpus has none;
  the report warns if a future one does rather than quietly inflating.

HUMAN TIME vs MACHINE TIME
  A call is human-gated when its tool_result record's permissionDecision.source begins
  with `user_` (user_temporary / user_permanent / user_reject) — a human answered a
  prompt. AskUserQuestion is human time by definition. Both are reported apart from
  tool cost, never inside it.
  This total is NOT attention and NOT compute. It is the wall clock between the prompt
  being raised and someone answering, so a session left open overnight contributes the
  whole night. The report prints the longest single wait next to the sum for exactly
  that reason: read them together or not at all.

BLOCKING WAIT
  A Bash call for which claude/blocking-wait-nudge.sh fires when replayed: a loop
  keyword paired with a sleep, an explicit `gh run watch` / `gh pr checks --watch`, or a
  bare `sleep N` with N >= 30. Backgrounded calls are exempt (the hook's own exemption).
  Cost is split, mirroring the header's "4.17h standalone + 8.68h embedded in gh/push":
    standalone — the wait is the whole command;
    embedded   — the matching command also mentions `gh ` or `push`, so the wait is a
                 clause inside work that had to happen anyway.
  Splitting on command text is this tool's guess at what "embedded" meant.

FULL-SUITE RUN, AND A REPEAT
  A full-suite run is a Bash call for which claude/full-suite-nudge.sh's predicate
  matches. That hook only writes its state file when the predicate matched, so the run
  count is read from the hook's own `runs` counter rather than re-implemented here.
  A REPEAT is the 2nd-or-later full-suite run within one session. Edits since the
  previous full run counts Edit/Write/NotebookEdit tool_use blocks between them, which
  is what the hook's PostToolUse arm counts. A repeat SURFACED A FAILURE when its
  tool_result is flagged is_error (a non-zero exit). That is narrower than the header's
  "surface a real failure" and will undercount a suite that fails while exiting 0.

HOOK FIRE RATE
  Two different things, never added together:
    MEASURED — Stop hooks are logged. Each `system`/`stop_hook_summary` record is one
      Stop event and lists every hook that ran with its own durationMs. Fires and cost
      are exact, not inferred.
    REPLAYED — PreToolUse and PostToolUse fires are NOT logged anywhere, which is why
      the headers speak of "replaying it over the corpus". Each recorded tool_input is
      rebuilt into a hook payload and fed to the real hook; a non-empty stdout is a
      fire. Session-scoped gates (once-per-session, edit-streak re-arm) replay correctly
      because CLAUDE_NUDGE_STATE is pointed at a scratch dir, so the hooks keep their
      own state exactly as they do in production.
  A hook's `if:` filter is applied before the hook runs, as Claude Code applies it:
  a segment of the command (split on && || ; |) whose leading word matches.
  claude/stop-hook-git-check.sh is deliberately NOT replayed: it inspects the live
  working copy, so replaying it would measure this checkout, not the corpus.

STOP EVENT (for replaying a Stop hook)
  The last assistant text block before each human turn, plus the final one in the file.
  Compare the replayed count against the logged stop_hook_summary count in the report:
  they measure the same thing two ways, and a large gap means this rule is wrong.

INSTALLED vs USED
  installed: enabledPlugins set true in settings.json; hook commands configured there;
    SKILL.md files under --skills-root, counted as distinct `plugin:skill` ids because
    the installed tree stores the same skill once per install layer (marketplace clone,
    one cache dir per resolved version, and the repo source of a local plugin);
    agent definitions in <hooks-dir>/agents.
  attributed: a plugin or skill appears as attributionPlugin / attributionSkill on an
    assistant record (the work happened under it), or as a Skill tool call; an agent
    appears as agentType in a subagent .meta.json sidecar; a plugin is also credited for
    a `plugin:skill`, a `plugin:agent` or an `mcp__plugin__tool` naming it.
  ATTRIBUTED IS NOT FIRED, and the gap is not small. Attribution requires a plugin's
  NAME to appear in some record, which happens only for those surfaces. Therefore the
  following run without ever being credited, and land in the never-named list:
    - a hook-only plugin. Claude Code logs the hook command with ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}
      unexpanded, so the fire is measured but unattributable. In the corpus this tool
      was written against, one such hook fired on all 45 Stop events while its plugin
      was reported unused; the report now prints that count beside the list.
    - an output style, a statusline, an LSP server, or a plugin whose value is a
      CLAUDE.md fragment: no tool call and no attribution field ever names them.
    - a plugin last used before the window. Retention is 30 days, so "never named here"
      means "not in these 32 transcripts", never "not used".
  Read the never-named list as a list of CANDIDATES to look at, not as a verdict.
  A skill inventory that cannot be read is reported as unknown, never as zero. Passing
  --skills-root REPLACES the default roots rather than adding to them.
DEFS
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- arguments
CORPUS_ROOT=""
CONFIG=""
HOOKS_DIR=""
SKILL_ROOTS=()
LEDGER=""
DO_REPLAY=1
AS_JSON=0

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --config)      CONFIG=${2:-};      shift 2 ;;
    --hooks-dir)   HOOKS_DIR=${2:-};   shift 2 ;;
    --skills-root) SKILL_ROOTS+=("${2:-}"); shift 2 ;;
    --no-replay)   DO_REPLAY=0;        shift ;;
    --json)        AS_JSON=1;          shift ;;
    --definitions) definitions; exit 0 ;;
    --ledger)
      if [ -n "${2:-}" ] && case "${2:-}" in --*) false ;; *) true ;; esac; then
        LEDGER=$2; shift 2
      else
        LEDGER="$REPO_DIR/docs/claude-corpus-ledger.tsv"; shift
      fi ;;
    -h|--help)     usage; exit 0 ;;
    --*)           die "unknown flag: $1 (see --help)" ;;
    *)             [ -n "$CORPUS_ROOT" ] && die "unexpected argument: $1"
                   CORPUS_ROOT=$1; shift ;;
  esac
done

need jq
need find

CORPUS_ROOT=${CORPUS_ROOT:-$HOME/.claude/projects}
# jq and grep read a leading '-' as a flag, and EVERY project dir under
# ~/.claude/projects is a cwd with '/' replaced by '-' — so every path in this corpus
# begins with a dash unless it is absolute. Absolutise once, here.
case "$CORPUS_ROOT" in /*) ;; *) CORPUS_ROOT="$PWD/$CORPUS_ROOT" ;; esac
[ -d "$CORPUS_ROOT" ] || die "corpus root not found: $CORPUS_ROOT"

# The repo copy is preferred over ~/.claude for both config and hooks: it is the
# version-controlled source of truth, and it keeps a run over a frozen corpus
# reproducible. Installed into the store, <repo>/claude does not exist and the live
# config is used instead -- which is the right answer there.
if [ -z "$CONFIG" ]; then
  if [ -r "$REPO_DIR/claude/settings.json" ]; then CONFIG="$REPO_DIR/claude/settings.json"
  else CONFIG="$HOME/.claude/settings.json"; fi
fi
if [ -z "$HOOKS_DIR" ]; then
  if [ -d "$REPO_DIR/claude" ]; then HOOKS_DIR="$REPO_DIR/claude"
  else HOOKS_DIR="$HOME/.claude"; fi
fi
# The repo marketplace holds only the five locally-authored plugins (13 SKILL.md). The
# surface whose descriptions sit in EVERY system prompt is the installed tree, ~71 files
# -- so defaulting to the repo copy alone undercounted the one number this tool exists to
# report. Both are default now. The installed tree is layered (a marketplace clone, plus
# one cache dir per resolved version, plus the repo source of the local plugins), so the
# same skill is found three times; the inventory counts distinct plugin:skill ids and
# reports the file count separately rather than scanning one layer and missing the rest --
# superpowers' 14 skills exist ONLY under cache/, so excluding it would report the most
# used plugin in the corpus as having no skills installed.
if [ ${#SKILL_ROOTS[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
  [ -d "$REPO_DIR/claude/marketplaces" ] && SKILL_ROOTS+=("$REPO_DIR/claude/marketplaces")
  [ -d "$HOME/.claude/plugins" ] && SKILL_ROOTS+=("$HOME/.claude/plugins")
fi

tmp=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/claude-corpus.XXXXXX") || die "cannot create temp dir"
trap 'rm -rf -- "$tmp"' EXIT INT TERM

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- jq preludes
# Timestamps are ISO-8601 with a millisecond fraction, which fromdateiso8601 refuses
# outright ("does not match format %Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"). Parse the fraction separately.
PRELUDE='
def ep:
  if type != "string" then null
  else (try capture("^(?<d>[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})(?:\\.(?<f>[0-9]+))?Z?$") catch null) as $m
  | if $m == null then null
    else (try (($m.d + "Z") | fromdateiso8601) catch null) as $s
    | if $s == null then null
      else $s + (if ($m.f // "") == "" then 0 else ("0." + $m.f | tonumber) end) end
    end
  end;
def segs: (if type == "string" then . else "" end)
  | [splits("&&|\\|\\||;|\\|")]
  | map(sub("^[[:space:](]+"; ""));
def leads: segs | map((try (capture("^(?<w>[^[:space:]]+)").w) catch "") | sub("^.*/"; ""));
def txt: if type == "string" then . else ([.[]? | select(type == "object" and .type == "text") | .text] | join("\n")) end;
'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- discovery
find "$CORPUS_ROOT" -type f -name '*.jsonl' >"$tmp/all.list" 2>/dev/null
grep -c . "$tmp/all.list" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
n_files=$(grep -c . "$tmp/all.list" 2>/dev/null || true)
[ "${n_files:-0}" -gt 0 ] || die "no *.jsonl transcripts under $CORPUS_ROOT"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pass A: facts
# One jq per transcript rather than one global slurp: memory stays bounded by the
# largest single transcript, and a transcript jq cannot parse becomes a counted skip
# instead of a dead run.
FACTS_JQ='
[inputs] as $L
| ([$L[] | select(length > 0)] | length) as $nz
| [$L[] | select(length > 0) | (fromjson? // empty)] as $R
| (reduce ($R[] | select(.type == "user")) as $r ({};
    reduce ($r.message.content | arrays | .[] | select(.type == "tool_result")) as $tr (.;
      .[$tr.tool_use_id] = {
        ts: $r.timestamp,
        err: ($tr.is_error == true),
        src: ($r.permissionDecision.source // null),
        dec: ($r.permissionDecision.decision // null),
        # toolUseResult is sometimes a bare string. `objects` would yield empty here,
        # and an empty field deletes the whole enclosing object in jq -- silently
        # dropping the record instead of erroring. Never filter by type inside a
        # constructed field; branch on it.
        interrupted: ((($r.toolUseResult // {})
                       | if type == "object" then (.interrupted == true) else false end))
      }))) as $res
| [$R[] | select(.type == "assistant") | . as $a
   | ($a.message.content | arrays | map(select(.type == "tool_use")) | length) as $batch
   | ($a.message.content | arrays | .[] | select(.type == "tool_use"))
   | ($res[.id] // {}) as $r
   | {
       tool: .name, id: .id, batch: $batch,
       sid: ($a.sessionId // "?"),
       dur: (if $r.ts == null then null else (($r.ts | ep) - ($a.timestamp | ep)) end),
       err: ($r.err // false), src: ($r.src // null), dec: ($r.dec // null),
       interrupted: ($r.interrupted // false),
       skill: ((.input // {}) | if type == "object" then (.skill // .command // null) else null end),
       agent: ((.input // {}) | if type == "object" then (.subagent_type // null) else null end)
     }] as $calls
| {
    file: $file, kind: $kind,
    lines: $nz, unparsed: ($nz - ($R | length)),
    sessions: ([$R[].sessionId // empty] | unique),
    t_min: ([$R[].timestamp // empty] | min), t_max: ([$R[].timestamp // empty] | max),
    versions: ([$R[].version // empty] | unique),
    cwds: ([$R[].cwd // empty] | unique),
    calls: $calls,
    batches_over_1: ([$calls[] | select(.batch > 1)] | length),
    hookfires: [$R[] | select(.subtype == "stop_hook_summary") | .hookInfos | arrays | .[]
                | {cmd: (.command // "?"), ms: (.durationMs // 0)}],
    stops: [$R[] | select(.subtype == "stop_hook_summary")
            | {prevented: (.preventedContinuation == true),
               reason: ((.stopReason // "") | length > 0),
               errs: (.hookErrors | arrays | length)}],
    turns: [$R[] | select(.subtype == "turn_duration") | .durationMs // 0],
    cost: [$R[] | select(.type == "cost-state")
           | {sid: (.sessionId // "?"), toolMs: (.totalToolDuration // 0),
              totalMs: (.totalDuration // 0), apiMs: (.totalAPIDuration // 0),
              usd: (.totalCostUSD // 0)}],
    skillmsgs: [$R[] | .attributionSkill // empty],
    pluginmsgs: [$R[] | .attributionPlugin // empty],
    skillcmds: [$R[] | .toolUseResult | objects | .commandName // empty],
    denials: [$R[] | .toolDenialKind // empty]
  }
'
: >"$tmp/facts.jsonl"
unreadable=0
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  kind=top
  case "$f" in */subagents/*) kind=sub ;; esac
  if ! jq -R -n -c --arg file "$f" --arg kind "$kind" "$PRELUDE$FACTS_JQ" \
        "$f" >>"$tmp/facts.jsonl" 2>"$tmp/jqerr"; then
    unreadable=$((unreadable + 1))
    printf '%s: skipped unreadable transcript %s (%s)\n' \
      "$SELF" "$f" "$(head -1 "$tmp/jqerr" 2>/dev/null)" >&2
  fi
done <"$tmp/all.list"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pass B: events
# The replay needs events in transcript order, because the full-suite gate is driven by
# edits that happened BETWEEN two runs.
#
# join("\t"), never @tsv: @tsv escapes backslashes, which turns the payload's own \"
# into \\" and hands the hook unparsable JSON. The hook then reads an empty command and
# exits 0, so a predicate that fires perfectly well is reported as never firing.
# tojson has already escaped every tab and newline, so the record stays single-line.
EVENTS_JQ='
[inputs] as $L
| [$L[] | select(length > 0) | (fromjson? // empty)] as $R
| (reduce ($R[] | select(.type == "assistant" or (.type == "user" and .isMeta != true))) as $r
     ({last: null, out: []};
      if $r.type == "assistant" then
        ([$r.message.content | arrays | .[] | select(.type == "text") | .text] | join("\n")) as $t
        | if ($t | length) > 0 then .last = $t else . end
      else
        (($r.message.content | arrays | [.[] | select(.type == "tool_result")] | length) // 0) as $ntr
        | if (($r.message.content | type) == "string" or $ntr == 0)
          then (if .last != null then .out += [.last] | .last = null else . end)
          else . end
      end)
   | .out + (if .last != null then [.last] else [] end)) as $stops
| ([$R[] | select(.type == "assistant") | . as $a
    | ($a.message.content | arrays | .[] | select(.type == "tool_use"))
    | {sid: ($a.sessionId // $file), tool: .name, id: .id, input: (.input // {})}]) as $calls
| ( ($calls[] | select(.tool == "Bash")
     | (.input | if type == "object" then (.command // "") else "" end) as $c
     | [ $kind, .sid, "pre_bash", (.id // "-"),
         (if ($c | leads | any(. == "grep" or . == "rg")) then "1" else "0" end),
         (if ($c | segs | any(test("^gh[[:space:]]+api\\b"))) then "1" else "0" end),
         ({hook_event_name: "PreToolUse", session_id: .sid, tool_name: "Bash",
           tool_input: .input} | tojson) ] | join("\t")),
    ($calls[] | select(.tool == "Edit" or .tool == "Write" or .tool == "NotebookEdit")
     | [ $kind, .sid, "post_edit", (.id // "-"), "0", "0",
         ({hook_event_name: "PostToolUse", session_id: .sid, tool_name: .tool,
           tool_input: .input} | tojson) ] | join("\t")),
    ($stops[] | [ $kind, (($R[0].sessionId) // $file), "stop", "-", "0", "0",
         ({hook_event_name: "Stop", session_id: (($R[0].sessionId) // $file),
           stop_hook_active: false, last_assistant_message: .} | tojson) ] | join("\t")) )
'
: >"$tmp/events.tsv"
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  kind=top
  case "$f" in */subagents/*) kind=sub ;; esac
  jq -R -n -r --arg file "$f" --arg kind "$kind" "$PRELUDE$EVENTS_JQ" "$f" \
    >>"$tmp/events.tsv" 2>/dev/null
done <"$tmp/all.list"

# Derived independently of the replay, so the "logged vs derived" stop-event check still
# cross-validates the turn-boundary rule under --no-replay. Subagents are excluded: a
# subagent ending raises SubagentStop, not Stop, so counting them would make the check
# disagree with the log for a reason that has nothing to do with the rule being tested.
stop_events=$(grep -c $'^top\t[^\t]*\tstop\t' "$tmp/events.tsv" 2>/dev/null)
stop_events=${stop_events:-0}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- replay
# A hook's own state dir is redirected into the scratch tree, so once-per-session and
# edit-streak gates replay exactly as they gate in production without touching the live
# ~/.cache/claude-nudge.
: >"$tmp/verdicts.tsv"
replay_elapsed=0
replay_runs=0
hooks_missing=""
if [ "$DO_REPLAY" -eq 1 ]; then
  H_BW="$HOOKS_DIR/blocking-wait-nudge.sh"
  H_FS="$HOOKS_DIR/full-suite-nudge.sh"
  H_AG="$HOOKS_DIR/ast-grep-nudge-hook.sh"
  H_GW="$HOOKS_DIR/gh-api-write-guard.sh"
  H_SP="$HOOKS_DIR/stop-phrase-guard.sh"
  for h in "$H_BW" "$H_FS" "$H_AG" "$H_GW" "$H_SP"; do
    [ -r "$h" ] || hooks_missing="$hooks_missing ${h##*/}"
  done
  export CLAUDE_NUDGE_STATE="$tmp/nudge"
  mkdir -p "$CLAUDE_NUDGE_STATE"
  t0=$(date +%s)

  fs_runs_of() { # the hook's own counter; it is written only when the predicate matched
    local sid=$1 file runs rest
    file="$CLAUDE_NUDGE_STATE/$(printf '%s' "$sid" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_')"
    [ -r "$file" ] || { printf '0\n'; return; }
    read -r runs rest <"$file" 2>/dev/null
    printf '%s\n' "${runs:-0}"
  }

  while IFS=$'\t' read -r fkind sid ev tid is_grep is_ghapi payload; do
    [ -n "${ev:-}" ] || continue
    bw=0; fsm=0; fsn=0; ag=0; gw=0; sp=0
    case "$ev" in
      pre_bash)
        [ -r "$H_BW" ] && [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_BW" 2>/dev/null)" ] && bw=1
        if [ -r "$H_FS" ]; then
          before=$(fs_runs_of "$sid")
          out=$(printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_FS" 2>/dev/null)
          after=$(fs_runs_of "$sid")
          [ "$after" -gt "$before" ] 2>/dev/null && fsm=1
          [ -n "$out" ] && fsn=1
        fi
        if [ "$is_grep" = "1" ] && [ -r "$H_AG" ]; then
          [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_AG" 2>/dev/null)" ] && ag=1
        fi
        if [ "$is_ghapi" = "1" ] && [ -r "$H_GW" ]; then
          [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_GW" 2>/dev/null)" ] && gw=1
        fi
        replay_runs=$((replay_runs + 2)) ;;
      post_edit)
        [ -r "$H_FS" ] && printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_FS" >/dev/null 2>&1
        replay_runs=$((replay_runs + 1)) ;;
      stop)
        # Only top-level transcripts: a subagent ending is SubagentStop, and the Stop
        # hooks are not wired to it, so replaying them there would invent fires.
        [ "$fkind" = "top" ] || continue
        if [ -r "$H_SP" ]; then
          [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$payload" | bash "$H_SP" 2>/dev/null)" ] && sp=1
        fi
        replay_runs=$((replay_runs + 1)) ;;
    esac
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' \
      "$ev" "$tid" "$bw" "$fsm" "$fsn" "$ag" "$gw" "$sp" "$is_grep" >>"$tmp/verdicts.tsv"
  done <"$tmp/events.tsv"
  replay_elapsed=$(( $(date +%s) - t0 ))
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inventories
jq -n --slurpfile cfg "$CONFIG" '
  ($cfg[0] // {}) as $c
  | {
      plugins_enabled: [($c.enabledPlugins // {}) | to_entries[] | select(.value == true) | .key],
      plugins_disabled: [($c.enabledPlugins // {}) | to_entries[] | select(.value != true) | .key],
      hooks_configured: [($c.hooks // {}) | to_entries[] | .key as $ev | .value[]?
                         | (.matcher // "") as $m | .hooks[]?
                         | {event: $ev, matcher: $m, cmd: (.command // "?"), if: (.["if"] // null)}],
      allow: (($c.permissions.allow // []) | length),
      ask: (($c.permissions.ask // []) | length),
      deny: (($c.permissions.deny // []) | length)
    }' >"$tmp/inventory.json" 2>/dev/null \
  || printf '{"plugins_enabled":[],"plugins_disabled":[],"hooks_configured":[],"allow":0,"ask":0,"deny":0}\n' >"$tmp/inventory.json"

: >"$tmp/skills.list"
if [ ${#SKILL_ROOTS[@]} -gt 0 ]; then
  find "${SKILL_ROOTS[@]}" -type f -name 'SKILL.md' >>"$tmp/skills.list" 2>/dev/null
fi
: >"$tmp/agents.list"
find "$HOOKS_DIR/agents" -type f -name '*.md' >>"$tmp/agents.list" 2>/dev/null

# A subagent's .meta.json sidecar already names the agent type that ran, which is the
# only record of agent usage: the parent's Agent tool_use does not always name one.
find "$CORPUS_ROOT" -type f -name 'agent-*.meta.json' >"$tmp/meta.list" 2>/dev/null
: >"$tmp/agentuse.json"
if [ -s "$tmp/meta.list" ]; then
  jq -R -n -c '[inputs] as $f | [$f[] | select(length > 0)]' "$tmp/meta.list" >/dev/null 2>&1
  jq -s -c '[.[] | {agent: (.agentType // "?"), depth: (.spawnDepth // 0),
                    shape: (.requestShape // "?")}]' \
     $(grep . "$tmp/meta.list" 2>/dev/null) >"$tmp/agentuse.json" 2>/dev/null \
    || printf '[]\n' >"$tmp/agentuse.json"
else
  printf '[]\n' >"$tmp/agentuse.json"
fi
[ -s "$tmp/agentuse.json" ] || printf '[]\n' >"$tmp/agentuse.json"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- aggregate
jq -n \
  --slurpfile inv "$tmp/inventory.json" \
  --slurpfile agentuse "$tmp/agentuse.json" \
  --rawfile skills "$tmp/skills.list" \
  --rawfile agents "$tmp/agents.list" \
  --rawfile verdicts "$tmp/verdicts.tsv" \
  --arg corpus "$CORPUS_ROOT" --arg config "$CONFIG" --arg hooksdir "$HOOKS_DIR" \
  --argjson unreadable "${unreadable:-0}" \
  --argjson replay "$DO_REPLAY" \
  --argjson replay_runs "${replay_runs:-0}" \
  --argjson replay_elapsed "${replay_elapsed:-0}" \
  --argjson stop_events "${stop_events:-0}" \
  --arg hooks_missing "${hooks_missing# }" \
  --slurpfile facts "$tmp/facts.jsonl" '
def nz: . // 0;
def tally(f): [f] | group_by(.) | map({k: .[0], n: length}) | sort_by(-.n);
def p50: if length == 0 then null else (sort | .[((length - 1) / 2) | floor]) end;
def sum: if length == 0 then 0 else add end;
def basename: sub("^.*/"; "");
# A SKILL.md path names its plugin one or two components above the skill dir, depending
# on install layer: <mp>/plugins/<plug>/skills/<skill> in a marketplace clone or the repo
# source, <mp>/<plug>/<version>/skills/<skill> in the resolved cache. Identity must be
# plugin:skill, matching the attributionSkill spelling -- a bare skill name would merge
# two plugins that happen to ship the same skill name.
def skill_id:
  sub("/SKILL\\.md$"; "")
  | if test("/skills/") then
      (sub("^.*/skills/"; "")) as $name
      | (sub("/skills/[^/]+$"; "")) as $pre
      | ($pre | basename) as $p1
      | ($pre | sub("/[^/]+$"; "") | basename) as $p2
      | (if $p2 == "plugins" then $p1 else $p2 end) + ":" + $name
    else basename end;

($facts) as $F
| ($inv[0] // {}) as $INV
| ([$skills | split("\n")[] | select(length > 0)]) as $SKFILES
| ([$agents | split("\n")[] | select(length > 0)]) as $AGFILES
| ([$verdicts | split("\n")[] | select(length > 0) | split("\t")
    | {ev: .[0], tid: .[1], bw: (.[2] == "1"), fsm: (.[3] == "1"),
       fsn: (.[4] == "1"), ag: (.[5] == "1"), gw: (.[6] == "1"), sp: (.[7] == "1"),
       grepish: (.[8] == "1")}]) as $V
| ([$V[] | select(.ev == "pre_bash") | {key: .tid, value: .}] | from_entries) as $VB
| ([$F[] | .calls[]]) as $CALLS
| ([$CALLS[] | select(.tool != "AskUserQuestion")]) as $TOOLCALLS
| ([$TOOLCALLS[] | select((.src // "") | startswith("user_"))]) as $GATED
| ([$CALLS[] | select(.tool == "Bash")]) as $BASH
| ([$BASH[] | select($VB[.id].bw == true)]) as $BW
| ([$BASH[] | select($VB[.id].fsm == true)]) as $FS
# cost-state is written repeatedly as a session runs and each record is CUMULATIVE, so
# summing the records multiplies one session by how often it was snapshotted. Reduce to
# the high-water mark per session first.
| ([$F[] | .cost[]] | group_by(.sid)
   | map({sid: .[0].sid, toolMs: ([.[] | .toolMs] | max), totalMs: ([.[] | .totalMs] | max),
          apiMs: ([.[] | .apiMs] | max), usd: ([.[] | .usd] | max)})) as $COST
# A plugin that ships agents or MCP tools instead of skills is never named by
# attributionPlugin, so attribution alone reports jj-master unused while its agents ran.
# Credit a plugin for any of its named surfaces.
| (([$F[] | .pluginmsgs[]])
   + ([$F[] | .skillmsgs[]] | map(select(test(":")) | sub(":.*$"; "")))
   + ([($agentuse[0] // [])[] | .agent] | map(select(test(":")) | sub(":.*$"; "")))
   + ([$CALLS[] | .tool | select(startswith("mcp__")) | sub("^mcp__"; "") | sub("__.*$"; "")])
   | unique) as $PLUGUSED
| (($INV.plugins_enabled // []) | map(sub("@.*$"; ""))) as $PLUGENABLED
| ($SKFILES | map(skill_id) | unique) as $SKIDS
| ([$F[] | .skillmsgs[]] + [$F[] | .skillcmds[]] | unique) as $SKATTR
| {
  corpus: {
    root: $corpus, config: $config, hooks_dir: $hooksdir,
    files: ($F | length),
    files_top: ([$F[] | select(.kind == "top")] | length),
    files_sub: ([$F[] | select(.kind == "sub")] | length),
    files_unreadable: $unreadable,
    lines: ([$F[] | .lines] | sum),
    lines_unparsed: ([$F[] | .unparsed] | sum),
    sessions: ([$F[] | .sessions[]] | unique | length),
    projects: ([$F[] | .cwds[]] | unique | length),
    window_start: ([$F[] | .t_min // empty] | min),
    window_end: ([$F[] | .t_max // empty] | max),
    versions: ([$F[] | .versions[]] | unique)
  },
  tools: {
    calls: ($CALLS | length),
    calls_top: ([$F[] | select(.kind == "top") | .calls[]] | length),
    calls_sub: ([$F[] | select(.kind == "sub") | .calls[]] | length),
    unpaired: ([$CALLS[] | select(.dur == null)] | length),
    interrupted: ([$CALLS[] | select(.interrupted)] | length),
    errors: ([$CALLS[] | select(.err)] | length),
    parallel_batches: ([$F[] | .batches_over_1] | sum),
    machine_s: ([$TOOLCALLS[] | select(.src == null or ((.src // "") | startswith("user_") | not)) | .dur // 0] | sum),
    human_gated_n: ($GATED | length),
    human_gated_s: ([$GATED[] | .dur // 0] | sum),
    # Reported because the total is dominated by its tail: a prompt raised at midnight and
    # answered after breakfast is 7h of UNATTENDED wall clock, not 7h of human attention
    # and not one second of compute. Without the max, the sum reads as a cost figure.
    human_gated_max_s: ([$GATED[] | .dur // 0] | max),
    ask_user_n: ([$CALLS[] | select(.tool == "AskUserQuestion")] | length),
    ask_user_s: ([$CALLS[] | select(.tool == "AskUserQuestion") | .dur // 0] | sum),
    by_tool: ([$CALLS | group_by(.tool)[]
               | {tool: .[0].tool, n: length,
                  s: ([.[] | .dur // 0] | sum),
                  p50: ([.[] | select(.dur != null) | .dur] | p50),
                  max: ([.[] | .dur // 0] | max)}] | sort_by(-.s)),
    denials: tally($F[] | .denials[]),
    decisions: tally($CALLS[] | select(.src != null) | .src),
    # Subagent records carry the PARENT sessionId, so a session and the subagents it
    # spawned group together -- which is also how they share one hook state file.
    by_session: ([$CALLS | group_by(.sid)[]
                  | {sid: .[0].sid, n: length,
                     bash: ([.[] | select(.tool == "Bash")] | length),
                     s: ([.[] | .dur // 0] | sum),
                     err: ([.[] | select(.err)] | length)}] | sort_by(-.bash))
  },
  waits: {
    fires: ($BW | length),
    bash_calls: ($BASH | length),
    total_s: ([$BW[] | .dur // 0] | sum),
    max_s: ([$BW[] | .dur // 0] | max),
    p50_s: ([$BW[] | select(.dur != null) | .dur] | p50)
  },
  suite: {
    runs: ($FS | length),
    nudges: ([$BASH[] | select($VB[.id].fsn == true)] | length),
    total_s: ([$FS[] | .dur // 0] | sum),
    failures: ([$FS[] | select(.err)] | length),
    sessions_with_runs: ([$FS[] | .sid] | unique | length)
  },
  astgrep: {
    candidate_calls: ([$V[] | select(.ev == "pre_bash" and .grepish)] | length),
    fires: ([$V[] | select(.ag)] | length),
    native_grep_calls: ([$CALLS[] | select(.tool == "Grep" or .tool == "Glob")] | length)
  },
  ghguard: { fires: ([$V[] | select(.gw)] | length) },
  stophooks: {
    logged_events: ([$F[] | .stops[]] | length),
    derived_events: $stop_events,
    prevented: ([$F[] | .stops[] | select(.prevented)] | length),
    with_reason: ([$F[] | .stops[] | select(.reason)] | length),
    phrase_guard_replay_fires: ([$V[] | select(.sp)] | length),
    by_hook: ([[$F[] | .hookfires[]] | group_by(.cmd)[]
               | {cmd: .[0].cmd, fires: length, ms: ([.[] | .ms] | sum)}] | sort_by(-.fires))
  },
  turns: {
    n: ([$F[] | .turns[]] | length),
    total_s: (([$F[] | .turns[]] | sum) / 1000),
    max_s: (([$F[] | .turns[]] | max // 0) / 1000)
  },
  cost: {
    sessions_reporting: ($COST | length),
    logged_tool_s: (([$COST[] | .toolMs] | sum) / 1000),
    logged_total_s: (([$COST[] | .totalMs] | sum) / 1000),
    logged_api_s: (([$COST[] | .apiMs] | sum) / 1000),
    usd: ([$COST[] | .usd] | sum)
  },
  config: {
    plugins_enabled: ($INV.plugins_enabled // [] | length),
    plugins_disabled: ($INV.plugins_disabled // [] | length),
    plugins_used: $PLUGUSED,
    plugins_unused: (($INV.plugins_enabled // []) | map(sub("@.*$"; "")) - $PLUGUSED),
    # Claude Code logs a plugin hook command with ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} UNEXPANDED, so a
    # fire proves a plugin ran but never says which one. Counting these is the only honest
    # way to bound the error in `plugins_unused`: a hook-only plugin lands there having run.
    plugin_hook_fires: ([$F[] | .hookfires[] | select(.cmd | test("CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT"))] | length),
    skills_installed: ($SKIDS | length),
    skills_installed_files: ($SKFILES | length),
    skills_installed_names: $SKIDS,
    # SKILL.md on disk is not SKILL.md in the system prompt: a disabled plugin stays
    # installed, and this tree holds several. Only the enabled subset is a context cost,
    # so that is the one the report leads with -- the disk count without it reads as a
    # bigger dead-weight claim than the config actually makes.
    skills_enabled: ($SKIDS | map(select((sub(":.*$"; "")) as $p | $PLUGENABLED | index($p)))),
    # Built-in skills carry no plugin prefix and live in no --skills-root, so they appear
    # in neither list; this counts INSTALLED plugin skills only.
    skills_unattributed: ($SKIDS - $SKATTR),
    skills_unattributed_enabled:
      (($SKIDS | map(select((sub(":.*$"; "")) as $p | $PLUGENABLED | index($p)))) - $SKATTR),
    skills_used: (tally($F[] | .skillmsgs[])),
    skill_calls: (tally($F[] | .skillcmds[])),
    agents_installed: ($AGFILES | map(sub("\\.md$"; "") | basename)),
    agents_used: ([($agentuse[0] // [])[] | .agent] | group_by(.) | map({k: .[0], n: length}) | sort_by(-.n)),
    hooks_configured: ($INV.hooks_configured // []),
    allow_rules: ($INV.allow // 0), ask_rules: ($INV.ask // 0), deny_rules: ($INV.deny // 0)
  },
  replay: {
    enabled: ($replay == 1),
    hook_invocations: $replay_runs,
    elapsed_s: $replay_elapsed,
    hooks_missing: $hooks_missing
  }
}' >"$tmp/metrics.json" || die "aggregation failed"

[ -s "$tmp/metrics.json" ] || die "aggregation produced nothing"

if [ "$AS_JSON" -eq 1 ]; then
  cat "$tmp/metrics.json"
  exit 0
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- report
jq -r '
def pad($n): tostring | . + ((" " * (($n - length) | if . < 1 then 0 else . end)) // "");
def lp($n): tostring | (((" " * (($n - length) | if . < 1 then 0 else . end)) // "") + .);
def hms: if . == null then "-"
  elif . >= 3600 then ((. / 3600 * 100 | round / 100 | tostring) + "h")
  elif . >= 60 then ((. / 60 * 10 | round / 10 | tostring) + "m")
  else ((. * 100 | round / 100 | tostring) + "s") end;
def ratio($n; $d): if ($d // 0) == 0 then "-" else (($n / $d * 1000 | round / 10 | tostring) + "%") end;

"claude-corpus — \(.corpus.root)",
"",
"CORPUS",
"  \(.corpus.files) transcript(s): \(.corpus.files_top) session, \(.corpus.files_sub) subagent" +
  (if .corpus.files_unreadable > 0 then "  (\(.corpus.files_unreadable) unreadable, skipped)" else "" end),
"  \(.corpus.lines) records, \(.corpus.sessions) session id(s), \(.corpus.projects) project dir(s)" +
  (if .corpus.lines_unparsed > 0 then ", \(.corpus.lines_unparsed) unparsable line(s) skipped" else "" end),
"  window  \(.corpus.window_start // "?") .. \(.corpus.window_end // "?")",
"  client  \(.corpus.versions | join(", "))",
"",
"WHAT IS INSTALLED BUT NEVER ATTRIBUTED IN THIS WINDOW",
"  plugins   \(.config.plugins_enabled) enabled, \(.config.plugins_used | length) named by a record in this window",
(if (.config.plugins_unused | length) > 0 then
  "            never named: \(.config.plugins_unused | join(", "))" else empty end),
(if (.config.plugin_hook_fires // 0) > 0 then
  "            NOT \"never fired\": \(.config.plugin_hook_fires) logged hook fire(s) came from a plugin",
  "            via ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}, which the log leaves unexpanded — so the plugin that",
  "            ran cannot be named from the transcript and is listed above as never named."
 else empty end),
(if (.config.skills_installed) > 0 then
  "  skills    \(.config.skills_enabled | length) from an ENABLED plugin, of \(.config.skills_installed) installed under --skills-root",
  "            (\(.config.skills_installed_files) SKILL.md on disk: the installed tree keeps one copy per install layer)",
  "            \(.config.skills_unattributed_enabled | length) of the enabled ones were never attributed in this window; \(.config.skills_used | length) skill(s) were"
 else
  "  skills    inventory not enumerated (pass --skills-root); \(.config.skills_used | length) attributed here"
 end),
(if (.config.skills_used | length) > 0 then
  (.config.skills_used[] | "              \(.k | pad(38)) \(.n | lp(5)) assistant msg(s)")
 else "              (none)" end),
(if (.config.skill_calls | length) > 0 then
  (.config.skill_calls[] | "              \(.k | pad(38)) \(.n | lp(5)) Skill invocation(s)")
 else empty end),
"  agents    \(.config.agents_installed | length) defined in hooks dir, \(.config.agents_used | length) type(s) spawned",
(if (.config.agents_used | length) > 0 then
  (.config.agents_used[] | "              \(.k | pad(38)) \(.n | lp(5)) subagent run(s)")
 else empty end),
"  rules     \(.config.allow_rules) allow / \(.config.ask_rules) ask / \(.config.deny_rules) deny",
"",
"TOOL CALLS   (wall clock = tool_result timestamp - tool_use timestamp)",
"  \(.tools.calls) call(s): \(.tools.calls_top) in sessions, \(.tools.calls_sub) in subagents",
"  machine time        \(.tools.machine_s | hms)   (auto-approved calls, AskUserQuestion excluded)",
"  human approval      \(.tools.human_gated_s | hms)   over \(.tools.human_gated_n) call(s) a human had to approve",
"                      longest single wait \(.tools.human_gated_max_s | hms) — unattended wall clock, not compute",
"  AskUserQuestion     \(.tools.ask_user_s | hms)   over \(.tools.ask_user_n) call(s) — human think time, not cost",
"  unpaired            \(.tools.unpaired) call(s) with no recorded result (counted, zero time)",
"  errored             \(.tools.errors) is_error, \(.tools.interrupted) interrupted",
(if .tools.parallel_batches > 0 then
  "  WARNING  \(.tools.parallel_batches) call(s) share a start timestamp with a sibling: per-call time is double-counted"
 else "  (no assistant message carried >1 tool_use block, so no concurrent-call double-count)" end),
"",
"  per tool, ELAPSED (approval waits included — compare `machine time` above, not this)",
"  \("tool" | pad(20)) \("calls" | lp(6)) \("total" | lp(9)) \("p50" | lp(9)) \("max" | lp(9))",
(.tools.by_tool[] | "  \(.tool | pad(20)) \(.n | lp(6)) \(.s | hms | lp(9)) \(.p50 | hms | lp(9)) \(.max | hms | lp(9))"),
"",
"  per session (subagent calls counted under the parent session they ran for)",
"  \("session" | pad(20)) \("calls" | lp(6)) \("bash" | lp(6)) \("elapsed" | lp(9)) \("is_error" | lp(9))",
(.tools.by_session[] | "  \(.sid[0:18] | pad(20)) \(.n | lp(6)) \(.bash | lp(6)) \(.s | hms | lp(9)) \(.err | lp(9))"),
"",
"  logged cross-check: cost-state over \(.cost.sessions_reporting) session(s) reports",
"    totalToolDuration \(.cost.logged_tool_s | hms), totalAPIDuration \(.cost.logged_api_s | hms), wall \(.cost.logged_total_s | hms), $\(.cost.usd * 100 | round / 100)",
"",
"HOOKS — MEASURED (Stop events are the only ones Claude Code logs)",
"  \(.stophooks.logged_events) logged stop event(s); the turn-boundary rule derives \(.stophooks.derived_events)",
"  \(.stophooks.prevented) prevented continuation, \(.stophooks.with_reason) carried a stopReason",
(if (.stophooks.by_hook | length) > 0 then
  (.stophooks.by_hook[] | "    \(.cmd | pad(46)) \(.fires | lp(5)) fire(s)  \(.ms / 1000 | hms | lp(8)) in-hook")
 else "    (no stop_hook_summary records in this corpus)" end),
"",
(if .replay.enabled then
  "HOOKS — REPLAYED (PreToolUse/PostToolUse fires are not logged; the real hooks were run)",
  "  \(.replay.hook_invocations) hook invocation(s) in \(.replay.elapsed_s)s" +
    (if (.replay.hooks_missing | length) > 0 then "   MISSING: \(.replay.hooks_missing)" else "" end),
  "  blocking-wait-nudge   \(.waits.fires | lp(5)) fire(s) over \(.waits.bash_calls) Bash call(s)   cost \(.waits.total_s | hms) (max \(.waits.max_s | hms))",
  "  full-suite-nudge      \(.suite.nudges | lp(5)) nudge(s) over \(.suite.runs) full-suite run(s) in \(.suite.sessions_with_runs) session(s), cost \(.suite.total_s | hms)",
  "                        \(.suite.failures) of \(.suite.runs) exited non-zero (\(ratio(.suite.failures; .suite.runs)))",
  "  ast-grep-nudge        \(.astgrep.fires | lp(5)) fire(s) after the once-per-session gate, from \(.astgrep.candidate_calls) leading grep/rg call(s)",
  "  gh-api-write-guard    \(.ghguard.fires | lp(5)) ask(s) forced",
  "  stop-phrase-guard     \(.stophooks.phrase_guard_replay_fires | lp(5)) fire(s) over \(.stophooks.derived_events) derived stop event(s)",
  "  native Grep/Glob tool calls in corpus: \(.astgrep.native_grep_calls)"
 else
  "HOOKS — REPLAYED: skipped (--no-replay)"
 end),
"",
"TURNS",
"  \(.turns.n) logged turn duration(s), total \(.turns.total_s | hms), longest \(.turns.max_s | hms)",
"",
"Run with --definitions for the exact rule behind every number above."
' "$tmp/metrics.json"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ledger
# Long format -- one row per metric, never a column per metric. A new metric appends
# rows instead of rewriting every historical line, so the diff of a run is the run.
if [ -n "$LEDGER" ]; then
  mkdir -p "$(dirname -- "$LEDGER")" 2>/dev/null
  if [ ! -s "$LEDGER" ]; then
    {
      printf '# claude-corpus ledger: one row per metric per run, append-only.\n'
      printf '# The transcript corpus is deleted after 30 days; these rows outlive it.\n'
      printf '# Re-derive any row with: claude-corpus --definitions\n'
      printf 'run_utc\twindow_start\twindow_end\tfiles\tsessions\tmetric\tvalue\n'
    } >"$LEDGER"
  fi
  jq -r --arg run "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" '
    # Bound to $d, never to `.`: the per-tool and per-hook rows are generated inside an
    # iteration where `.` is one element, so reading the window off `.` wrote rows with an
    # empty window and no file count — unreadable the moment the corpus they describe is
    # deleted, which is the one failure this ledger exists to prevent.
    . as $d
    | def row($m; $v): [$run, ($d.corpus.window_start // "?" | .[0:10]),
                        ($d.corpus.window_end // "?" | .[0:10]),
                        $d.corpus.files, $d.corpus.sessions, $m, $v] | @tsv;
      row("corpus.records"; .corpus.lines),
      row("corpus.unparsed"; .corpus.lines_unparsed),
      row("tools.calls"; .tools.calls),
      row("tools.machine_s"; .tools.machine_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("tools.human_gated_s"; .tools.human_gated_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("tools.human_gated_max_s"; .tools.human_gated_max_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("tools.askuser_s"; .tools.ask_user_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("tools.errors"; .tools.errors),
      row("cost.logged_tool_s"; .cost.logged_tool_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("cost.usd"; .cost.usd | . * 100 | round / 100),
      row("astgrep.native_grep_calls"; .astgrep.native_grep_calls),
      row("stop.logged_events"; .stophooks.logged_events),
      row("stop.prevented"; .stophooks.prevented),
      # A --no-replay run knows nothing about fire rates. Writing 0 for them would put a
      # measurement in the ledger that was never made, and the ledger is the artifact
      # nobody can re-derive once the corpus expires.
      (if .replay.enabled then
        row("waits.fires"; .waits.fires),
        row("waits.total_s"; .waits.total_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
        row("suite.runs"; .suite.runs),
        row("suite.nudges"; .suite.nudges),
        row("suite.failures"; .suite.failures),
        row("suite.total_s"; .suite.total_s | . * 100 | round / 100),
        row("astgrep.fires"; .astgrep.fires),
        row("astgrep.leading_grep_calls"; .astgrep.candidate_calls),
        row("ghguard.fires"; .ghguard.fires),
        row("stop.phrase_guard_fires"; .stophooks.phrase_guard_replay_fires)
       else empty end),
      row("config.plugins_enabled"; .config.plugins_enabled),
      row("config.plugins_used"; .config.plugins_used | length),
      row("config.plugin_hook_fires"; .config.plugin_hook_fires),
      row("config.skills_installed"; .config.skills_installed),
      row("config.skills_installed_files"; .config.skills_installed_files),
      row("config.skills_enabled"; .config.skills_enabled | length),
      row("config.skills_unattributed"; .config.skills_unattributed | length),
      row("config.skills_unattributed_enabled"; .config.skills_unattributed_enabled | length),
      row("config.skills_used"; .config.skills_used | length),
      row("config.agents_used"; .config.agents_used | length),
      ($d.tools.by_tool[] | row("tool." + .tool + ".calls"; .n)),
      ($d.tools.by_tool[] | row("tool." + .tool + ".s"; .s | . * 100 | round / 100)),
      # A logged hook command is a shell fragment, not a path: `bash "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}
      # /hooks/stop-hook.sh"` kept its closing quote in the metric name. Strip anything that
      # cannot be in a filename so a metric key stays greppable across runs.
      ($d.stophooks.by_hook[]
       | row("hook." + (.cmd | sub("^.*/"; "") | gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9._-]"; "")) + ".fires"; .fires)),
      ($d.config.skills_used[] | row("skill." + .k + ".msgs"; .n)),
      ($d.config.agents_used[] | row("agent." + .k + ".runs"; .n))
  ' "$tmp/metrics.json" >>"$LEDGER" \
    && printf '\n%s: appended %s metric row(s) to %s\n' \
         "$SELF" "$(grep -c . "$LEDGER")" "$LEDGER"
fi
